    "prompt": "TASK\nReplace the green placeholder character in @video1 with the person shown in @image1. Perform a consistent full-character replacement throughout the entire video while preserving the source footage’s choreography, shot sequence, timing, camera movement, environment, vehicle and soundtrack.\n\nREFERENCES\n@video1 = master reference for all motion, performance, framing, editing, scene geometry, lighting, physical interactions and audio. The green character identifies the performer to replace.\n\n\n@image1 = replacement character identity and appearance. Preserve the recognizable face, facial structure, skin tone, hair, apparent age, body characteristics and visible outfit. Do not transfer the reference image’s background, pose, camera angle or lighting. Reconstruct unseen angles consistently with the visible appearance.\n\nCHARACTER REPLACEMENT\nReplace the complete green placeholder, including the head, face, neck, torso, arms, hands, legs, feet and any placeholder clothing. The final performer must look like the person shown in @image1, naturally filmed within the original scene.\n\nUse the green character’s movement as the performance guide, not as the final appearance. Adapt the replacement character’s proportions naturally while preserving the original seated position, screen placement, movement paths and contact points.\n\nPreserve the original facial performance and gaze direction, expressed through the replacement character’s recognizable face. Do not copy a static expression from @image1.\n\nPERFORMANCE & CHOREOGRAPHY\nMatch every action to the corresponding source frame:\n— Remain seated at the chess table while the vehicle hovers overhead.\n— Maintain the same body lean, shoulder position and placement of the resting arm.\n— Reach for, grip, lift, move and place the same chess pieces at the same moments.\n— Preserve the exact hand trajectories, finger contacts and release timing.\n— Continue the calm, concentrated performance during the vehicle’s descent and the empty chair’s fall.\n— Complete the final chess move.\n— Bring the hand toward the lips or chin at the original moment.\n— Preserve the restrained smile and subsequent head turn toward the parked vehicle.\n— Hold the final pose through the vehicle’s headlight wink.\n\nDo not add gestures, dialogue, exaggerated reactions or new actions.\n\nHAIR, CLOTHING & PHYSICAL INTEGRATION\nHair and clothing follow @image1. Animate them according to the source scene’s downwash, movement and gravity. Adapt wind response to the replacement character’s actual hairstyle and outfit rather than reproducing the placeholder’s silhouette.\n\nKeep hands physically aligned with the chess pieces and tabletop. Maintain convincing seated contact with the chair and natural placement of the feet.\n\nMatch the source lighting on the replacement character: cool propulsion light during the hover, changing illumination during shutdown, and headlights with red background light after landing. Update the character’s contact shadows and any visible reflections to match the new appearance.\n\nLeave no green skin, green edges, placeholder body parts, green color spill or traces of the original character.\n\nSCENE & EDITING LOCK\nPreserve the source video’s duration, aspect ratio, shot boundaries, camera angles, camera movement, focus changes, motion blur and final transition to black.\n\nKeep the vehicle exactly as it appears in @video1, including its body, paint, wheels, propulsion jets, flight path, descent, landing, headlights and final amber flash.\n\nPreserve the table, chessboard, chess pieces, chairs, chair fall, vapor, wet pavement, background lights and environmental motion. Change only the performer and the shadows, reflections or occlusions directly affected by the replacement.\n\nAUDIO\nPreserve the original audio from @video1 unchanged, with identical synchronization, music, vocals, sound effects, levels and ending. Do not regenerate the soundtrack or add dialogue, Foley, ambience or new sounds. Do not make the replacement character lip-sync to background vocals.\n\nQUALITY & CONTINUITY\nMaintain the same recognizable replacement character across every shot, camera distance, head turn and partially obscured frame. Preserve facial features, hairstyle, outfit and body proportions consistently.\n\nNo identity drift, flickering, ghosting, doubled faces, extra limbs, distorted fingers, floating hands or clipping through scene objects. No changes to the vehicle or background. No additional cuts, captions, logos or watermarks.\n\nFINAL RESULT\nThe source video plays with the same actions, timing, cinematography and sound, with the person shown in @image1 seamlessly replacing the green placeholder throughout."
