    "prompt": "CREATIVE MAPPING\n@image1 = The sole identity reference for the ballerina. Preserve the exact facial features, facial proportions, skin tone, hair color, hairline, hair texture and hairstyle shown in @image1. Do not replace the hairstyle with a generic ballet bun. Use the ballet costume described below.\n\nONE-SENTENCE SUMMARY\nA photorealistic vertical 9:16, 10-second continuous shot of the woman from @image1 as a classical ballerina, performing exactly four graceful full turns while the camera slowly pulls back from a close-up of her pristine pointe shoes to reveal her full figure, finishing balanced en pointe.\n\nGLOBAL SETTINGS\nIDENTITY\nThe same recognizable woman from @image1 throughout the shot. Maintain her facial identity and exact hairstyle in frontal, profile and rear views. Preserve natural skin texture.\n\nWARDROBE\nA pristine white ballet leotard with thin straps and a flat, structured white classical tutu. Pale blush ballet tights and spotless pale-pink satin pointe shoes with neatly crossed ribbons. Realistic satin highlights and delicate tulle texture.\n\nANKLE TRACKER\nExactly one matte-black electronic ankle tracker, with a compact rectangular housing and a black strap, secured just above the right ankle bone, over the tights and above the shoe ribbons. It stays attached to the same anatomical right leg throughout every turn. No device on the left leg.\n\nBODY AND FEET\nAn elongated upright posture, relaxed shoulders and straight, closely held legs. Both feet remain fully en pointe, close together in a slightly crossed position.\n\nThe right supporting pointe maintains uninterrupted contact with the same small area of the floor. The shoe rotates in place together with the leg and body; it is not locked against rotation. The other pointe stays close beside it, tracing a small, smooth arc along the floor without visible lifting or stepping.\n\nThe feet, knees, hips and torso turn together in alignment. No isolated twisting of the torso over an immobile leg. Maintain a steady body height without bouncing.\n\nARMS\nBoth arms softly rounded in front of the waist, elbows gently held outward, hands separated, wrists relaxed and fingers naturally curved. Maintain this composed position throughout, without raising the arms overhead.\n\nTURN COUNT AND PACING\nExactly four complete 360-degree turns across the entire 10-second shot, all in the same direction. Two complete turns during the first five seconds, followed by two complete turns during the final five seconds. These are four turns total, not additional turns in each description.\n\nDistribute the movement across the full duration with a smooth, measured, unhurried cadence. No rapid spinning followed by a long pause. Finish the fourth turn facing the starting direction, balanced en pointe, with heels still raised.\n\nENVIRONMENT AND LIGHTING\nA seamless white studio cyclorama and clean matte white floor. Broad, soft high-key lighting with delicate contact shadows beneath the pointe shoes. Preserve detail in the white costume, natural skin tones and subtle satin reflections. Photorealistic live-action fashion cinematography.\n\nCAMERA\nOne uninterrupted shot. Begin close to floor level with a tight close-up of both pointe shoes, their contact with the floor and the ankle tracker. Slowly dolly backward and rise, progressively revealing the tutu, rounded arms and face. Finish with the entire figure visible, including the shoes and space above the head. No camera orbit. Smooth focus adjustment as the framing widens.\n\nTIMELINE\n[0s-5s]\nStart on the pointe-shoe close-up and begin the continuous backward-and-upward camera move. The ballerina completes the first two full turns. At the end of this interval, the cumulative rotation count is exactly two. The supporting pointe maintains floor contact throughout.\n\n[5s-10s]\nContinue the same camera move and uninterrupted turning motion. The ballerina completes the remaining two full turns, bringing the cumulative total to exactly four. The framing reaches full body. At the end, she finishes facing the starting direction and remains elegantly balanced on the tips of her pointe shoes, arms rounded in front of her waist, without lowering her heels.\n\nAUDIO REQUIREMENTS\nSubtle studio ambience and faint fabric movement only. No music or speech.\n\nNEGATIVES\nExtra rotations, rapid spinning, stepping or shuffling, jumps, lifted supporting foot, lowered heels, raised overhead arms, anatomical distortion, identity or hairstyle changes, duplicated or migrating ankle tracker, dirty pointe shoes, camera shake, cuts, flicker, text, watermarks."
