    "prompt": "Use @image1 as the exact reference.\n\n15-second vertical video, 9:16, 24fps. A single unbroken close-up. Shot like a 2000s beauty commercial — the glossy cosmetics-ad aesthetic of that era: soft diffused beauty light, flawless luminous skin, long defined lashes, glossy lips, clean high-key framing, a slight glow bloom on the highlights, gentle film grain and a very soft focus falloff at the edges. Photorealistic, real human skin with visible texture and pore detail under the light. No modern filters, no HDR look, no crisp digital sharpness — warm, soft, shot-on-film feel.\n\nFRAMING — one continuous take, no cuts, no angle changes, camera never moves position. Tight close-up, three-quarter angle on the woman from 3/4 front-left. She is seated at a dressing table, phone held up in one hand at face level, tapping the screen with her thumb. Over her shoulder, clearly inside the frame from the very first frame and staying there the whole time, hangs a large mirror with a plain frame. The mirror is visible the entire video. Her reflection is visible in it from the start, soft in the background.\n\nLIGHT — one single consistent setup for all 15 seconds: a large soft key light in front of her giving that flawless even beauty-ad glow, a gentle rim from behind separating her hair, and a warm practical lamp visible in the background next to the mirror. The light never changes, never shifts, never restarts — this is the same continuous shot.\n\nSHE IS TAPPING THROUGH HALLOWEEN FILTERS. Each change happens live on her face, smoothly and realistically — like a real AR filter switching. Her real face stays underneath every time; only the makeup, skin and styling transform. The filters are bold and vivid, each one a strong instant change, styled like the heavy Halloween filter packs that go viral.\n\n0–2 sec: VAMPIRE — bold and vivid. Her skin flashes porcelain white with a cold blue undertone, eyes go deep crimson with a dark rim, sharp white fangs slide down over her lips, dark liner sharpens the gaze, a blood-red shadow pools under her cheekbones. Dramatic, vampiric, instantly striking. The phone screen throws a cool red-white glow up onto her face. She tilts her chin, pleased.\n\n2–5 sec: WITCH — bold and vivid. Her appearance transforms into a vivid glamorous witch while remaining fully recognizable as the same real woman. Deep emerald and violet light hits her face, black flowing fabric rises across her shoulders, heavy smoky eye makeup, dark plum lips, and a real physical black pointed witch hat appears on her head as part of the WITCH filter. The same physical hat is naturally visible on both the real woman and her reflection in the mirror. The hat is a real three-dimensional object worn directly on her head, with a visible brim and tall pointed crown. It is NOT a shadow, NOT a projection, and NOT an effect on the wall. The hat exists ONLY during the 2–5 second WITCH filter. Faint unnatural green vapour curls around her head. Her eyes flash a bright unnatural green for a beat. She turns her head slightly, checking herself in the phone, pleased with the transformation.\n\n5–8 sec: ZOMBIE — bold and vivid. The WITCH filter is completely replaced by the ZOMBIE filter. The physical witch hat disappears completely from the real woman's head and from her reflection before the zombie appearance is fully established. No hat remains anywhere in the scene. Grey-green decayed skin with fine cracking, veins and mottled bruising, cloudy milky eyes with no iris left, broken dark teeth, dried dark lips, torn clothing at her shoulder, grime in the creases of her skin. Cold sickly green light washes over her face from the screen. Despite all of it she is still lit like a beauty commercial and still striking. She studies the screen, almost amused.\n\n8–10 sec: SHE TURNS IT OFF. She taps once more. All Halloween filter elements disappear completely from both the real woman and her reflection. The witch hat, zombie makeup, zombie eyes, costume elements and all supernatural effects vanish entirely. Her normal appearance returns with no hat and no remaining Halloween elements. Warm light lands on her real face again. She laughs, warm and relieved, exhales, drops her shoulders. Her face in the foreground is completely normal — clean skin, natural makeup, her own eyes.\n\n10–13 sec: THE MIRROR — THE COPY IS NOT CLEAN. She is still holding the phone and looking directly at its screen. Her real face in the foreground is completely clean and human. In the mirror behind her, however, her reflection is STILL THE ZOMBIE — grey-green decayed skin, cloudy milky eyes, torn clothes. The reflection is out of sync with her real body. The real woman does NOT look at the mirror yet. She remains focused on her phone.\n\nThen the zombie reflection slowly turns its head toward her independently of her real body and looks directly at her. The reflection raises one finger to its lips.\n\nA clear whispered \"Shhh\" comes from the reflection behind her.\n\nThe instant she hears the \"Shhh\", BEFORE looking at the mirror, the real woman's expression changes. She stops looking at the phone and looks genuinely startled by the sound. Her eyes widen in sudden confusion and fear, her eyebrows lift, her smile disappears, and she freezes for a brief moment while still holding the phone. She clearly reacts to the SOUND first. She does not immediately know what caused it.\n\n13–15 sec: SHE TURNS AND SEES IT. Immediately after reacting to the \"Shhh\", she turns her head sharply toward the mirror, then quickly turns her upper body toward it. The movement is fast, instinctive and frightened. Only now does she see the terrifying zombie reflection directly. Her eyes become wide with shock, her mouth opens in disbelief, and she recoils.\n\nShe suddenly drops the phone from her hand. The phone falls naturally downward out of frame.\n\nShe screams once in genuine fear and immediately runs away from the dressing table, leaving the frame.\n\nIMPORTANT: her real body leaves the frame, but the zombie reflection DOES NOT leave the mirror. The zombie reflection remains standing or seated in exactly the same position inside the mirror, still facing the mirror and staring forward. It does not follow her movement, does not run away, does not disappear, and does not transform back. The mirror continues to contain the terrifying zombie reflection after the real woman has escaped.\n\nEnd on the empty foreground with the mirror still visible and the zombie reflection remaining alone inside it.\n\nSound design: total silence throughout the entire video. No music, no room tone, no ambience, no street noise, no phone UI sounds, no breathing, no fabric rustling.\n\nThe ONLY sounds are:\n\n1. One clear whispered \"Shhh\" spoken ONLY by the zombie reflection in the mirror.\n2. Immediately afterward, one sudden frightened scream from the real woman.\n\nThe \"Shhh\" must clearly happen BEFORE she turns toward the mirror. She reacts to hearing the sound first, then turns and discovers the terrifying reflection. The scream happens only after she has turned toward the mirror and visually seen the zombie reflection.\n\nThe phone makes no sound when it falls. No dialogue other than \"Shhh\" and her single scream."
