    "prompt": "Use @image1 as the exact reference for the woman throughout the entire video. Preserve her exact identity, facial features, face shape, eyes, nose, lips, hairstyle, hair color, skin tone, body proportions and overall appearance. She must remain the EXACT SAME WOMAN in all four Halloween looks. Only the costume, accessories, makeup styling, lighting and supernatural effects change. \n\n[CORE STYLE] 15-second HIGH-FASHION HALLOWEEN MUSIC VIDEO. Think luxury fashion campaign meets modern viral music video meets supernatural Halloween transformation. EXTREME HYPERREALISM. Everything must look like real professional live-action footage shot on a high-end cinema camera. The video must be FAST, DYNAMIC, STYLISH and RHYTHMIC. The MUSIC BEAT controls the editing. Every strong beat should trigger a visual change: a new camera angle, a whip-pan, a close-up, a body movement, a costume detail, a vortex hit or a full transformation. Do NOT hold the same camera angle for long. Use aggressive but elegant editorial cutting. Rapid cuts, match cuts, whip transitions, speed ramps, micro close-ups and unexpected camera angles. The result should feel like an expensive fashion campaign edited to a modern viral track. \n\n[CAMERA LANGUAGE] Constantly changing cinematic perspectives. Wide establishing shots. Extreme close-ups of eyes, lips, jewelry, hands and costume details. Low-angle fashion shots. High-angle shots. Side profiles. Three-quarter portraits. Camera extremely close to her body while she dances. Fast orbiting camera movements. Low camera tracking around her legs. Over-the-shoulder shots. Dutch angles. Rapid push-ins. Whip pans. Camera passing behind her body and revealing the next shot. Occasional 0.2–0.5 second beauty shots before immediately cutting again. Use different focal lengths for visual variety: wide-angle distortion for energetic moments, 50mm cinematic portraits, 85mm fashion close-ups. The camera should feel like a real fashion cinematographer is physically moving around the model. \n\n[EDITING] RAPID, RHYTHMIC, MUSIC-DRIVEN EDITING. Average shot duration approximately 0.3–1.0 seconds during the dance sections. Some ultra-short 2–5 frame inserts synchronized to percussion. Cuts happen exactly on the kick, snare, bass hit or musical accent. Never let the video feel slow or repetitive. Use: SMASH CUTS WHIP CUTS MATCH CUTS SPEED RAMPS RAPID PUSH-INS ROTATIONAL CUTS MOTION-MATCH TRANSITIONS CLOSE-UP INSERTS VORTEX TRANSITIONS The camera movement and editing should feel intentionally chaotic but still luxurious and controlled. \n\n[0–3.5 SEC — PUMPKIN QUEEN] Start immediately with a strong beat. 0.0–0.5 — Extreme close-up of her eyes. CUT. 0.5–1.0 — Low-angle shot of her heels stepping onto the marble floor. CUT ON BEAT. 1.0–1.5 — Close-up of her hand moving through her hair. CUT. 1.5–2.2 — Full-body fashion shot as she performs a confident dance movement. Camera rapidly orbits around her. 2.2–2.7 — Extreme close-up of the orange-and-black costume details. 2.7 — MUSIC DROP. She spins. A huge orange supernatural vortex suddenly explodes around her. The camera spins violently with the vortex. Orange particles, smoke, leaves and glowing embers fill the frame. SMASH CUT INTO THE NEXT LOOK. \n\n[3.5–6.5 SEC — WITCH] The vortex disappears directly on the beat. She is now the SAME WOMAN from @image1 wearing a high-fashion gothic witch outfit. Immediate rapid montage: Close-up of her eyes. CUT. Low-angle shot of the witch hat. CUT. Side profile as her hair moves through the air. CUT. Full-body dance shot. CUT. Extreme close-up of black lace and jewelry. CUT. Camera rotates 180 degrees around her. She continues the exact same dance choreography. Purple-black smoke moves around her. At 5.7 seconds she makes a sharp spin. The camera follows her rotation. The entire frame becomes a dark purple vortex. BEAT DROP. \n\n[6.5–9.5 SEC — VAMPIRE] SMASH CUT. The same woman appears as a high-fashion vampire. Black and deep-burgundy couture dress, velvet cape, lace, elegant jewelry. Immediately cut between: Extreme close-up of lips. Close-up of eyes. Low-angle fashion shot. Fast shot of cape moving. Side profile. Full-body dance. Camera rushing toward her face. She looks directly into the lens. CUT ON BASS HIT. Crimson-black vortex suddenly wraps around the camera. The camera rotates rapidly through the vortex. \n\n[9.5–12.5 SEC — DARK DEMON] Hard cut into the final transformation. Same woman. Dark demon / gothic devil fashion look. Black couture dress, subtle leather, dark red details, elegant curved black horns, smoky eyes, deep red lips. This section becomes the most visually intense. Rapid editorial montage: Extreme close-up of one eye. CUT. Low-angle shot of horns. CUT. Close-up of lips. CUT. Hand sliding across the costume. CUT. Camera circles her body at high speed. CUT. Wide fashion shot. CUT. Extreme close-up. CUT. She suddenly stops dancing and looks directly into camera. Music briefly drops out. Half-second silence. Then ONE MASSIVE BASS HIT. She gives a subtle, confident smile. \n\n[12.5–15 SEC — FINAL] Music returns with the final beat. Quick flashes of the four costumes — approximately 0.15–0.25 seconds each. PUMPKIN. WITCH. VAMPIRE. DEMON. Then instantly back to the final demon look. Camera rapidly pushes toward her face. Everything freezes for a fraction of a second. Text appears: “HAPPY HALLOWEEN” The typography is minimal, premium and fashion-editorial. \n\n[MODERN MUSIC — CRITICAL] Use a CURRENT, MODERN DARK POP / ELECTRONIC FASHION BEAT. The track must sound contemporary, rhythmic and addictive. Strong punchy KICK. Crisp SNARE. Deep SUB BASS. Sharp electronic percussion. Dark atmospheric synths. Subtle distorted vocal texture. Fashion runway energy mixed with modern club music. NO classical music. NO gothic waltz. NO slow horror soundtrack. The beat must be clearly audible and drive the entire edit. Every transformation and major camera cut must be synchronized to the music. Use bass drops specifically for the vortex transformations. Use short moments of silence immediately before major transformations to make the next beat hit harder. \n\n[SOUND DESIGN] Layer the music with precise cinematic sound design. Sharp camera whooshes during whip pans. Deep bass impacts during transformations. Fabric movement. Footsteps. Subtle supernatural wind. Vortex suction and rotation. Short impact sounds on major cuts. The sound design must enhance the rhythm without overpowering the music. \n\n[HYPERREALISM] EXTREME PHOTOREALISTIC LIVE-ACTION. Real human skin pores. Individual hair strands. Realistic eyes. Natural facial movement. Physically accurate clothing. Realistic fabric simulation. Natural hair physics. Realistic reflections. Realistic shadows. Physically accurate lighting. Real camera optics. Realistic motion blur. Natural depth of field. Subtle film grain. No plastic skin. No CGI-looking face. No cartoon. No anime. No game graphics. No artificial-looking VFX. No anatomy errors. No extra fingers. No face distortion. No identity drift. \n\n[FINAL RESULT] A 15-second HIGH-END HALLOWEEN FASHION MUSIC VIDEO. Fast. Sexy. Dark. Modern. Editorial. Hyperrealistic. Beat-driven. Visually unpredictable. Every second should contain something visually interesting. The viewer should feel that they are watching a luxury fashion campaign cut to a viral modern electronic track — with four spectacular Halloween transformations happening inside one continuous dance.\n\n"
